        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Ayacucho</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">9 December 1824</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Pampa de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> (also called Quinua), a few kilometres from the town of Quinua and from Ayacucho, in the Peruvian highlands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The United Liberation Army under Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a>, and the royalist army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> under Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-la-serna">José de la Serna</a>, with Lieutenant General José de Canterac as chief of the high command. Troop numbers are debated: the armies began with 8,500 independentists and 9,310 royalists, and on the day there were perhaps 5,780 independentists and 6,906 royalists.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The royalist army was broken, Viceroy La Serna was wounded and taken prisoner, and Canterac signed a capitulation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Sucre</a>. Sucre gives independentist losses as 370 killed and 609 wounded, and royalist losses as about 1,800 dead and 700 wounded.</span></div>
        </div>
