    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The royalists left a strong defensive position on high ground because they had food for less than five days and the Colombian reinforcements Bolívar expected would have made their position worse.</li><li>Troop numbers are debated: the armies began the campaign at 8,500 independentists and 9,310 royalists, but by the day of the battle there were perhaps 5,780 and 6,906.</li><li>The war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> ended with a capitulation, but fighting went on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> until April 1825 and the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> lasted until 1826.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Spanish historian Juan Carlos Losada has argued that the result had been agreed beforehand between commanders on both sides, a theory he admits can only be speculation because the main figures kept silent.</p></aside>
    </section>

